WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.910
When my plane landed here, the
flight attendant welcomed everyone

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:08.010
to West Yellowstone, Montana,
and added: "don't feed the bears".

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.139
There's a lot of reminders
not to do that.

00:00:10.139 --> 00:00:11.473
The whole of Yellowstone Park,

00:00:11.473 --> 00:00:14.540
which starts just a couple hundred
metres that way, is bear country.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.539
And hikers and campers heading
from this town into the wilderness

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:19.110
will bring food with them.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.039
If the wind's in the right direction, then
a bear can smell that food miles away.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:25.880
Which isn't great for the humans,
who do not want a hungry grizzly bear

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.100
that weighs a third of a ton rummaging
through and destroying their campsite.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.640
But it's not great for the bears either.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.510
Bears are relatively smart creatures,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.124
and if one of them makes
the connection between humans...

00:00:36.124 --> 00:00:37.879
and easily available food,

00:00:37.879 --> 00:00:40.410
it might start seeking out
campsites and homes,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.770
it might get aggressive.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.170
And in that case, it might
have to be... euthanized, killed.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:46.648
Not just for human safety, but because

00:00:46.648 --> 00:00:49.047
it might pass that behaviour
along to its cubs,

00:00:49.047 --> 00:00:51.220
and along to their cubs,
and suddenly,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.684
the wilderness isn't so wild anymore.

00:00:53.684 --> 00:00:55.125
So that's problem number one.

00:00:55.125 --> 00:00:58.135
There are rules for people going into
the park, and one of them involves

00:00:58.135 --> 00:01:02.671
keeping all your food in bear resistant
containers, a bit like these trash cans.

00:01:05.045 --> 00:01:06.320
So problem number two.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.510
How do you certify that a container
is actually bear resistant?

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.250
Here at the Grizzly and Wolf
Discovery Center, they're trying to

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.350
solve both those problems in one go.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.229
Rather than euthanizing a bear that's a
bit too interested in pick-a-nick baskets,

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:22.645
it can get moved here, just outside
the park, where it might get a new job:

00:01:22.645 --> 00:01:24.260
testing containers.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:29.140
- On average, our bears test around
60 products per season, which runs

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.910
from April 1st to October 1st.

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:35.070
Without a doubt, coolers are number one.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.750
30 to 40 coolers.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.279
Seems like everybody wants to make
a bear resistant cooler these days.

00:01:40.279 --> 00:01:43.260
So then we have the trash cans,
something you'd roll out

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.710
to the end of your driveway.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.670
Some metal products even get tested here.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:52.950
We want those products to pass the test so
that out in the field a bear doesn't get

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.770
that food reward and moves on, continues
back into the woods where they belong.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:59.820
Fish is their number one favorite.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.150
Peanut butter and honey is a close second.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.889
And then we either put dried
dog kibble or dog treats inside.

00:02:05.889 --> 00:02:08.500
Quite often we put a dummy container in,

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.556
To give those bears a food reward
every once in a while.

00:02:11.556 --> 00:02:17.160
If they were to not be able to get into
a product, 20 products in a row.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.359
Why, perhaps, would they try
to get in that 21st time?

00:02:21.359 --> 00:02:25.770
So let them remember that there's a
lot of goodies inside, and they need to

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.401
put that effort into trying to get in.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:31.830
- Definitely no bears
in here right now?

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.330
- No bears.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:38.559
Lots of double checks, security,
radio calls.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:39.465
- So obviously, I asked to put a camera

00:02:39.465 --> 00:02:41.656
inside one of the
dummy trash cans, 

00:02:41.656 --> 00:02:44.470
one of the trash cans that the bears
would definitely get into.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:47.415
I didn't think the team here would
let me, but they said yes immediately.

00:02:47.415 --> 00:02:49.600
And in hindsight,
of course they said yes.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.805
The bears are literally being given a
load of plastic and metal to rip apart

00:02:53.805 --> 00:02:55.819
in the hope of reaching the food inside.

00:02:55.819 --> 00:02:58.450
A camera in a protective case is not going to make any difference to them.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:02:59.630
They're not going to eat it.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:01.030
They're smart enough not to.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.070
- The bear's favorite method, at least
with the trash can, we find,

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:07.415
is the "CPR method", pushing
up and down with their paws,

00:03:07.415 --> 00:03:11.825
and a lot of times they
separate the lid from the body.

00:03:11.825 --> 00:03:16.794
With coolers, they're gonna use their
teeth to tear the plastic apart.

00:03:16.794 --> 00:03:20.495
And then the styrofoam,
and then the next layer, and then

00:03:20.495 --> 00:03:22.195
it's a clean-up on aisle four.

00:03:22.195 --> 00:03:24.404
- My big concern here was animal welfare.

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:28.315
There is a centuries-long history of bears
being mistreated for entertainment,

00:03:28.315 --> 00:03:30.585
and I do not want to be part of that.

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:34.534
This place has to survive as a non-profit,
and as a tourist attraction,

00:03:34.534 --> 00:03:37.435
but that can't mean putting
the bears' welfare at risk.

00:03:37.435 --> 00:03:40.475
Fortunately, the team here
are very clear that they don't

00:03:40.475 --> 00:03:42.044
want that to happen either.

00:03:42.044 --> 00:03:44.994
- We would never want to put
the bears at risk, for sure.

00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:47.774
These are all rescued bears
that came from the wild.

00:03:47.774 --> 00:03:49.294
We're very, very hands off.

00:03:49.294 --> 00:03:51.094
We don't call out to the bears.

00:03:51.094 --> 00:03:52.914
We don't talk to the bears.

00:03:52.914 --> 00:03:56.054
We want them to remain
as wild as possible.

00:03:56.054 --> 00:03:59.204
We also ask the public to
not call out to the bears.

00:03:59.204 --> 00:04:01.795
The bear habitat is about an acre in size.

00:04:01.795 --> 00:04:07.295
We do have two ponds, a waterfall,
and lots of trees and rocks and logs.

00:04:07.295 --> 00:04:10.445
We stock the pond with
fish all summer long.

00:04:10.445 --> 00:04:15.764
The food that's hidden out in the bear
habitat in front of the public is just

00:04:15.764 --> 00:04:17.995
an enrichment to keep them active.

00:04:17.995 --> 00:04:18.945
It's a snack.

00:04:18.945 --> 00:04:22.280
Let them do their natural behaviors
like they would in the wild.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.680
- It turns out that it's really
difficult to make a container that

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.010
humans will want to use, and can use,
but that bears can't get into.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.719
Yesterday, I tried to use one of those
bear resistant trash cans, and it

00:04:31.719 --> 00:04:34.679
had been jammed shut by someone that
apparently couldn't figure it out,

00:04:34.679 --> 00:04:36.920
and just shoved their
garbage in the latch.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.389
Because, there's an old quote, allegedly
from some park ranger in the 1980s,

00:04:40.389 --> 00:04:43.700
I couldn't find the original source,
that says, there's a considerable

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:48.100
overlap between the brainpower of
the most intelligent bears, and

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.469
the least intelligent tourists.

00:04:50.469 --> 00:04:52.590
- 60 minutes of contact time.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.960
Quarter inch hole or larger is a fail.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.540
We always say "bear resistant"
because we feel that if you give a

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:02.849
bear an unlimited amount of time,
they'll get in most everything.

00:05:02.849 --> 00:05:05.190
Not "bear proof", no guarantees.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:08.670
Those bears are gonna come around and try
to get that easy meal each and every time.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:12.418
We're trying to educate the visitors to
not make the same mistakes

00:05:12.418 --> 00:05:15.280
that got most of our bears here
in the first place.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.000
More and more bears are staying
out of trouble in the wild.

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:21.298
- Where's it gone?

00:05:23.312 --> 00:05:25.460
Oh yeah, no, that's a bear with a GoPro.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:26.924
That is a bear with a GoPro.

00:05:28.636 --> 00:05:31.299
Sadly, definitely not recording anymore.

00:05:31.299 --> 00:05:32.259
Oh, don't put it in the water.

00:05:32.259 --> 00:05:35.434
Don't... do not put the GoPro
in the water, bear. That's...

00:05:37.376 --> 00:05:38.279
And it's gone.

00:05:38.279 --> 00:05:39.609
That's... not ideal.

